## `length-zero`

Prefer `isempty(x)` or `!isempty(x)` when a length comparison tests whether a collection is empty. This includes `length(x) == 0`, `length(x) < 1`, and their reversed forms.

The safe fix preserves the argument and is withheld if `isempty` is shadowed or a comment would be lost.

Testing whether a collection is empty:

```julia
if length(xs) == 0
    println("nothing to do")
end
```

```text
warning: length-zero
 --> example.jl:1:4
  |
1 | if length(xs) == 0
  |    ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ test `isempty(xs)` instead of comparing `length(xs)` to `0`
  = help: Rewrite as an `isempty` test (safe fix)
```

After applying the fix:

```julia
if isempty(xs)
    println("nothing to do")
end
```

Testing whether a collection is nonempty:

```julia
while 0 < length(queue)
    pop!(queue)
end
```

```text
warning: length-zero
 --> example.jl:1:7
  |
1 | while 0 < length(queue)
  |       ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ test `!isempty(queue)` instead of comparing `length(queue)` to `0`
  = help: Rewrite as an `isempty` test (safe fix)
```

After applying the fix:

```julia
while !isempty(queue)
    pop!(queue)
end
```
